The sky above Oakhaven did not darken with ominous clouds; it simply turned a bruised purple, heavy with the scent of ozone and ancient stone. In the center of the village square sat Ignis, a dragon of colossal proportions, his scales the color of molten copper that had cooled into obsidian. He did not roar in anger; he exhaled with a contented sigh, sending a wave of heat that instantly wilted the wheat in the fields and turned the morning dew into steam.

The villagers, mostly children and elderly farmers who had forgotten how to run for their lives, watched in stunned silence. There was no panic, no screaming. As the flames touched the wooden rooftops, they didn't hiss in fear; they danced. The fire crackled with a rhythm that sounded like applause, turning the timber houses into living sculptures of orange and gold.

Ignis stretched his wings, feeling the warmth seep deep into his marrow. To him, this was not destruction;
